+Paper definition.  Section 2 defines K- and O-oriented supersingular elliptic
+curves and oriented isogenies.  Theorem 7 states that the ideal class group
+acts faithfully and transitively on primitive oriented classes.  Section 5.1
+writes, for O_i = Z + ell^i O_K and class-number-one O_K, the exact quotient
+
+  Cl(O_i) ~= (O_K/ell^i O_K)^* /
+             image(O_K^*) (Z/ell^i Z)^*.
+
+Section 5.2 makes a private key a bounded exponent vector (e_1,...,e_t) with
+|e_j| <= r on small oriented split primes.  Section 6 gives the parameter
+inequality and the concrete K=Q(i), ell=2 exponent-vector regime.  This module
+uses precisely those ideal classes and the quotient above; it does not compile
+elliptic curves into an unrelated graph.
+
+Step 0 and certificate cost.  The certificate is not produced by an SDP,
+linear solve, spectral formula, table, or polynomial-time root isolation.  It
+is sampled first and the target is its product, so generation is inverse.  A
+candidate is checked by at most t quotient-group multiplications.  Recovering
+one from the public product is bounded subset product after the quotient is
+linearized, with generic meet-in-the-middle cost (2r+1)^(t/2).  At shipping,
+this is 3^80.  The 2022 Dartois--De Feo attack invalidated the original paper's
+practical OSIDH parameter claim: it recovers extra public isogeny-chain data
+using lattice reduction and an exponential meet-in-the-middle phase.  This
+module therefore does not claim that OSIDH itself is secure.  Its Track-A claim
+is only for the explicit bounded exponent-recovery distribution, and its panel
+contains both a lattice/CVP probe and a meet-in-the-middle probe.
+
+Exact quotient model.  Put M=2^d.  A unit a+bi of Z[i]/M has exactly one odd
+coordinate.  Modulo Gaussian units {+1,-1,+i,-i} and odd scalar units, it has a
+unique representative 1+s*i with even s: use s=b/a when a is odd and s=-a/b
+when b is odd.  Thus two accumulated products can be compared without any
+floating point or class-group oracle.  Conjugation reverses an oriented prime
+and negates its exponent.  Each published a+bi has prime norm a^2+b^2 and is
+therefore the generator of one of the two oriented prime ideals above that
+split rational prime.
+
+Construction.  Draw the full exponent vector before drawing any primes.  Draw
+every Gaussian prime from one common bit interval and choose its conjugate with
+an independent fair bit.  Require every quotient class to have full 2-power
+order, a symmetric condition imposed on every coordinate, and reject duplicate
+classes.  The target is the product of those independently sampled classes to
+the already chosen exponents.  No coordinate is planted from a different
+distribution.
+
+Easy cases and attacks.  Tiny t is enumerable and is reserved for demo.  If
+the conductor depth or exponent dimension is small, dynamic programming,
+meet-in-the-middle, or lattice reduction can be practical.  The all-zero,
+norm-rank, greedy 2-adic-residual, small-support, and random-restart attacks
+probe generator leakage.  A capped meet-in-the-middle search and a rounded
+Kannan-embedding LLL/CVP projection probe the standard attacks.  Full BKZ/CVP,
+quantum hidden-shift algorithms, and an end-to-end implementation of the 2022
+OSIDH attack are not implemented; README.md records this limitation.
+
+Historical correction.  The paper's Theorem 7 is too broad as written for the
+entire set of primitive oriented curves; later work distinguishes two
+conjugate orbits.  The finite quotient identity and its multiplication law used
+here are unaffected, and this family never asks the verifier to decide orbit
+transitivity.
+""".strip()
